  "problem": "Which of the following shows some of the solution curves of the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{3}{2}(x+2)^{2}.$",
  "worked_solution": "Integrating the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{3}{2}(x+2)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =\\frac{3}{2}\\int (x+2)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(x+2)^{3}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plot the solution curves by selecting different values for $C.$ The sketch below shows the solution curves for $C=-1,0,1.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041209/assets/2ef13d3c7516974afcb94986ee9198b61d263f0ef53c344c02a5a8eb849e2c38.png)",
